      "text": "To determine how many positive factors of 96 are also multiples of 12, we first need to find the prime factorization of 96 and then identify the factors that are multiples of 12.\n\nThe prime factorization of 96 is:\n\\[ 96 = 2^5 \\times 3^1 \\]\n\nA factor of 96 must be of the form \\(2^a \\times 3^b\\) where \\(0 \\leq a \\leq 5\\) and \\(0 \\leq b \\leq 1\\). Additionally, for the factor to be a multiple of 12, \\(a\\) must be at least \\(1\\) (since 12 is \\(2^2 \\times 3^1\\), and to be a multiple of 12, \\(a\\) must include at least \\(2^1\\)) and \\(b\\) must be at least \\(1\\) (since 12 is \\(2^2 \\times 3^1\\), and to be a multiple of 12, \\(b\\) must include at least \\(3^1\\)).\n\nSo, \\(a\\) can be any value from 1 to 5 (which gives us 5 choices, namely 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5), and \\(b\\) can be any value from 1 to 1 (which gives us 1 choice, namely 1). Therefore, the total number of factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12 is:\n\\[ 5 \\times 1 = 5 \\]\n\nThus, the number of positive factors of 96 that are also multiples of 12 is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
